                  <provenance type="#e" cert="low"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_101">Cambridge,
                     Cambridgeshire. Jesus College</orgName><p><q>Liber m. Johannis gunthorp’ decani
                     Wellensis emptus London xx decembris ao xpi 1484to pro quindecim solidis
                     solutis</q> (inside front cover)</p><p><title>CBMLC</title> 10, 697 says that
                     this was given to Jesus by Gunthorpe, but on what evidence is not clear. Note
                     that James does not include Gunthorpe in his ‘List of donors of manuscripts’ (p
                     . 117), but in his ‘List of owners of manuscripts’ (ibid.). He thus does not
                     seem to have reached the conclusion drawn by <title>CBMLC</title>. Leland does
                     write of another MS (no longer extant), ‘Huius codicis possessor olim fuit
                     Gulielmus \Ioannes/ Gunthorp qui in Italia cum Frea bonis studuerat literis et
                     postea factus Decanus Wellensis, multos libros in Italia manu scriptos collegio
                     Iesu dono dedit.’ (<title>CBMLC</title> 10, 280). He also gave to Jesus CUL MS
                     Ff.6.20 (q.v.)</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_86">Bury St Edmunds,
                     Suffolk, Benedictine abbey of St Edmund, King and Martyr</orgName> <p>This
                     attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great
                     Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                     corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card records the
                     following information, which was deemed unsatisfactory for an attribution:
                     <q>The binding and the handwriting of the table of contents, as well as the
                     folio-numbering, recall very strongly the Bury MSS</q> (M. R.
                     James).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">John
                     Gunthorpe</persName> (d. 1498), dean of Wells, king’s almoner, and keeper of
                     the privy seal.</provenance>
               </history>
